Context Engineering for AI Coding Agents on a Sovereign Data Science Platform: Configuration and Evaluation for OpenCode on Onyxia / SSP Cloud
1. Introduction
Large-language-model coding agents have become genuinely effective. They no longer merely complete lines: they explore a codebase, run shell commands, edit files and orchestrate multi-step tasks, and recent evaluations on realistic software-engineering benchmarks show steady, substantial gains. For data scientists in official statistics the promise is broad, spanning the whole statistical production cycle — exploratory analysis, reproducible pipelines and the industrialisation of models.
Yet the same agent that performs well on a public repository often disappoints on an institutional platform. The reason is not capability but ignorance of the environment: out of the box, the agent reasons about a “generic cloud” and knows nothing about the concrete platform on which the data scientist actually works. National statistical institutes increasingly run their data science on specialised, sovereign platforms — at Insee this is Onyxia, deployed on the SSP Cloud, with its own object storage, secret management, experiment tracking, workflow orchestration, strong reproducibility requirements and strict confidentiality constraints. An agent that proposes provider-specific boilerplate, hard-codes credentials or downloads a dataset to local disk is, in this setting, at best unhelpful and at worst unsafe.
This abstract makes three moves. It argues that the decisive lever is context engineering, and that its value is realised only when the context is platform-specific. It presents opencode-onyxia [6], an open-source, reusable configuration that operationalises this for the OpenCode agent [4] on Onyxia / SSP Cloud [5], with all generation routed to self-hosted models so that no context leaves the institutional perimeter. And it sets out how such a configuration can actually be evaluated, through a benchmark harness we have built for the purpose.
2. Methodology
Context engineering is the deliberate design of everything a model sees beyond the immediate user prompt: persistent project context, role-specialised system prompts, on-demand domain know-how, tool and permission policies, and model routing. Whereas prompt engineering optimises the wording of a single instruction, context engineering curates a durable, reusable information environment for the agent [1]. We treat it operationally, as five complementary layers that turn a general-purpose agent into a competent “insider” of the platform. The configuration is a small set of version-controlled text files installed into the agent’s configuration directory, so that the encoded knowledge is shared and auditable rather than confined to documentation or practitioners’ heads.
2.1. The five layers
A single shared context file is loaded automatically for every agent. It makes explicit the platform’s operating model: its guiding principles; the services it provides (a Kubernetes / Helm catalogue, MinIO object storage with per-user buckets and a shared diffusion folder, Vault, MLflow, Argo Workflows / ArgoCD, duckdb); the environment variables injected at run time that must be read rather than hard-coded; the rule to ingest data directly from object storage into memory rather than downloading it; the expected toolchain (uv, ruff, pytest, polars / duckdb for Python; renv, targets, testthat for R; Quarto for reporting; nothing secret or data-bearing in Git); and a curated list of authoritative references [7,8,9].
Around it, four further layers. Role specialisation: primary planning and building agents plus sub-agents for Python and R data science, MLOps, chart reading, and a reviewer deliberately run on a different model for an independent viewpoint. On-demand skills: detailed procedures packaged as modules following the open Agent Skills standard [2], loaded only when a task matches their description — progressive disclosure, which keeps deep know-how instantly available without inflating the always-on context. Commands, which encapsulate recurring procedures such as scaffolding a reproducible project, scanning for leaked credentials, or running a diagnostic runbook. And guardrails with model routing: because the agent runs real shell commands and is not sandboxed, the permission policy requires explicit confirmation for network calls, Kubernetes mutations and deletions; remote tool servers (via the Model Context Protocol [3]) are disabled by default; and generation is routed by role across self-hosted models, balancing capability against cost while keeping context on-platform.
2.2. Why platform specificity matters
A single example illustrates why generic context is insufficient. On this platform the temporary object-storage token expires after seven days, after which every call returns an HTTP 403 error and the service is shown “in red”. The shared context instructs the agent to suspect token expiry first when it encounters such a 403 — a diagnosis a generic assistant would be unlikely to reach, and which it would otherwise pursue down misleading paths such as bucket policy or networking. Comparable platform knowledge is encoded so that the agent’s default behaviour matches the platform’s intended use.

