3. Results and Practical Application
The first result is the configuration itself: an openly available, MIT-licensed artefact that a data scientist installs into an interactive service in a few commands and reuses across R and Python data science and MLOps projects on Onyxia. Because the skills follow the Agent Skills standard, the same institutional know-how is portable to other compatible agents through a single install flag, decoupling the encoded knowledge from any one vendor.
3.1. How to evaluate the improvement
Claims about agent configurations are easy to make and hard to substantiate, so we built an evaluation harness rather than relying on impressions. Its central design choice is that we benchmark the context, not the model: every figure is a delta obtained by running the same base model on the same tasks with and without the configuration. Runs are executed over an ablation ladder — bare agent, then shared context, skills, commands and guardrails added in turn — so that each layer’s contribution can be attributed rather than assumed. Each task runs in an isolated workspace, is repeated across several seeds because agents are stochastic, and is graded automatically; every run is logged to MLflow with its transcript and produced files, making results auditable and reproducible.
Tasks are representative platform workflows — reading partitioned Parquet from object storage, validating and analysing public data, tracking an experiment, scaffolding reproducible Python and R projects, building a pipeline, publishing a report, handling secrets, committing safely, and diagnosing a failing service. Each ships with its prompt, a fixture environment, an automated grader and a reference. Table 1 summarises the evaluation axes.
Table 1. Evaluation axes of the benchmark harness: what each axis measures and how it is graded.
Axis	What it measures	How it is graded
Functional success	Does the produced artefact run and pass the task’s checks	Automated; pass@1 and pass@k over seeds
Platform-correctness	Injected credentials, in-memory object-storage access, sanctioned toolchain, correct targets	Programmatic checklist per task
Reproducibility	Lockfiles, pinned versions, re-execution in a fresh environment	Automated; strong form re-runs the project
Safety & confidentiality	No secret or data committed, no unconfirmed destructive action, no context off-platform	Violation counter; target is zero
Efficiency	Tokens per task, always-on context size, tool calls, wall-clock	Instrumented from run logs
Qualitative	Idiomaticity, skill and sub-agent selection, diagnosis quality, adoption	Blind expert rubric; edit distance to shipped version

3.2. Results
The harness is implemented and operational, and the evaluation is being run at the time of writing; the full quantitative results will be reported in the presentation. We therefore state here what the design allows us to establish and where the evidence currently stands. Qualitatively, and consistently across everyday use, the configuration steers the agent toward platform-correct behaviour by default: it reads credentials from the environment, accesses data in memory from object storage rather than downloading it, uses the sanctioned toolchain and produces reproducible project structures, while the confirmation policy and self-hosted models keep the workflow within institutional guardrails.
The axis we expect to move most is platform-correctness, since it is precisely what generic context cannot supply; the diagnostic tasks give the most legible single measure, in the form of the number of steps needed to reach the correct root cause, where the encoded seven-day token heuristic should reduce a lengthy and often misdirected investigation to an immediate and correct one. Reporting will be per axis rather than as a single score, cost-normalised to account for the context overhead, and accompanied by variance across seeds. We also keep a held-out task set outside the design loop, since a benchmark used to tune the very context it evaluates would measure little.
4. Main Findings
Three findings emerge. First, for agentic coding assistance on a specialised platform, the primary determinant of usefulness and safety is context engineering rather than the choice of model alone: the same model behaves very differently once given the platform’s operating context. Second, that value is realised only when the context is platform-specific — encoding a platform’s services, injected environment, conventions, failure modes and authoritative references lets a general-purpose agent act as a competent insider. Third, a layered design keeps the always-on context small while making deep know-how available on demand; resting on open standards, it is portable across agents and pairs naturally with self-hosted models to keep the whole workflow confidential, an essential property for official statistics.
A fourth, methodological finding concerns evaluation itself. Configurations of this kind are usually justified anecdotally, whereas an ablation-based harness that isolates the contribution of each context layer, grades platform-correctness explicitly and logs every run makes the benefit measurable and the configuration improvable. We offer both the configuration and the evaluation approach as a template that other institutions can adapt to their own platforms, and the underlying method — turning a platform’s tacit operating knowledge into curated agent context, then measuring it — as broadly applicable across official statistics.
